rsx draw engine: a 2D transfer over a target it does not update makes guest memory the newer copy.

Resistance 3: badly hurt, the screen went black but for a few blurred blobs (user report) instead of the blurred,
washed-out view. A pass dump of such a frame (RSX_ENGINE_PASS_DUMP, a draw per pass at the composite target, NV3089
copies logged between draws) shows the damage path: the frame at 0x019C0000 (pitch 4096) is copied with NV3089 to
0x01DA8800 at pitch 3840, and the next draw samples 0x01DA8800 as a 960x704 texture. That address also holds a
240x176 bloom target drawn at pitch 960 earlier in the frame and a shadow map's zeta. The transfer lands in neither
(guest_wrote updates only a surface laid out at its own pitch), so the engine still bound the bloom target, stretched,
for the copy.
Now guest_wrote marks every target its rectangle covers and does not update as older than guest memory (guest_newer),
until the target is created, drawn or cleared again. A texture unit skips marked targets (colour, zeta, the DEPTH24
snapshot fallback) and reads the guest bytes; a later transfer does not write a marked target back over them
(eng_surface_at skips it). A stateless rule (a linear texture whose pitch differs from the target's reads guest
memory) also fixed the frame but contradicts the depth-as-colour store (#499): a zeta written after the guest bytes must be stored and
re-read at the texture's pitch.

test_rsx_draw_engine, new test_transfer_supersedes_other_targets: a transfer at the target's own pitch keeps it
sampled; one at another pitch makes the unit upload from guest memory, keeps a later transfer from writing the old
pixels back, and a draw into the target makes it sampled again; a zeta covered by the transfer is not read back. Three
of its checks fail without the change. test_transfer_coherence wrote at another pitch over its surface before
checking that a transfer reads that surface's pixels; the two steps swap, every check kept. Checked with scripted
runs (Continue, stand in the Haven fight): the hurt frames show the blurred scene.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
